Ladybird resolves a cyclic percentage width of a text input or a
textarea against zero for its min-content contribution, as it does a
replaced box’s. The other engines all do the same. Blink does it in
ContentMinimumInlineSize(), for text controls and selects alike. Gecko
does it in nsIFrame::IsPercentageResolvedAgainstZero(), which covers
form controls. And WebKit does it in computeIntrinsicLogicalWidths()
of both RenderTextControl and RenderMenuList.

A percentage max-width doesn’t compress a text control, in Ladybird or
in the other engines: Gecko applies FormControlShrinksForPercentSize()
to the width only, and Blink keeps such a control at its default width.
